\label{ch:typed-ir}

A typed intermediate representation carries type information throughout the compilation pipeline. Types in the IR enable type-preserving compilation, where the type of every intermediate value is known and verified, and provide critical information for optimization. This chapter explores the design and benefits of typed IRs, from typed SSA to type-specialized compilation.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 61.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Why Types in IR?}\label{sec:why-types-ir}

Types in the IR serve multiple purposes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Correctness.} Type checking the IR catches compiler bugs (e.g., generating code that adds a pointer to a float).
  \item \textbf{Optimization.} Type information enables optimizations (e.g., using a 32-bit add instead of a 64-bit add for known-small values).
  \item \textbf{Verification.} Type-preserving compilation ensures that the compiled code respects the source language's type discipline.
  \item \textbf{Documentation.} Types make the IR self-documenting: the type of every value is explicit.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Typeless vs. Typed IR}\label{sec:typeless-vs-typed}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Typeless IR (three-address code):
  t1 = add t2, t3
  t4 = load t1

  // Typed IR (LLVM-like):
  %t1 = add i32 %t2, %t3
  %t4 = load i32*, i32 %t1
\end{verbatim}

The typed IR makes it explicit that the add operates on 32-bit integers and the load reads from a pointer to a 32-bit integer.

