\section{Type-Preserving Compilation}\label{sec:type-preserving}

\emph{Type-preserving compilation} ensures that the types of the source program are maintained throughout compilation: the compiled code has types that correspond to the source types.

\subsection{Definition}\label{sec:tpc-def}

\begin{definition}[Type-Preserving Compilation]\label{def:tpc}
A compiler is \emph{type-preserving} if there exists a type-erasure function $|\cdot|$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item If $\vdash e : \tau$ in the source, then $\vdash C(e) : |\tau|$ in the target IR.
  \item $|C(e)| = e$ (the compiled code, when types are erased, is the same as the source, when types are erased).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Benefits}\label{sec:tpc-benefits}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Safety.} Type-preserving compilation ensures that the compiled code cannot perform operations that the source type system prohibits.
  \item \textbf{Modularity.} Each compilation phase can be type-checked independently.
  \item \textbf{Certification.} Type-preserving compilers can produce certified code (proof-carrying code) where the type derivation serves as a certificate of correctness.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{TIL and Typed Closure Conversion}\label{sec:til}

The TIL (Typed Intermediate Language) compiler~\cite{morrisett1999} demonstrated type-preserving compilation from System~F to a typed assembly language (TAL). The compilation pipeline:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Source (System~F) $\to$ CPS (typed CPS) $\to$ Closure-converted (typed closures) $\to$ Hoisted (typed hoisted code) $\to$ TAL (typed assembly).
\end{enumerate}

Each phase preserves types, ensuring that the final assembly code is well-typed.

