\section{Typed SSA}\label{sec:typed-ssa}

Typed SSA combines the benefits of SSA form (explicit data flow) with type information.

\subsection{Type Rules for SSA}\label{sec:ssa-type-rules}

\begin{typerules}{Typed SSA}
  \typerule{Assign}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau}
            {\Gamma, x : \tau \vdash x = e : \mathrm{unit}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Phi}{\Gamma \vdash x_1 : \tau \quad \cdots \quad \Gamma \vdash x_n : \tau}
            {\Gamma, y : \tau \vdash y = \phi(x_1, \ldots, x_n) : \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Use}{\Gamma, x : \tau \vdash x : \tau}
            {\Gamma, x : \tau \vdash x : \tau}
\end{typerules}

The key constraint is that all arguments to a phi function must have the same type, and the phi function's result has that type.

\subsection{Type Checking SSA}\label{sec:ssa-type-checking}

Type checking SSA is straightforward:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item For each assignment $x = e$, check that $e$ has the declared type of $x$.
  \item For each phi function $y = \phi(x_1, \ldots, x_n)$, check that all $x_i$ have the same type as $y$.
  \item For each use of $x$, look up the type of $x$ in the environment.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{SSA and Type Inference}\label{sec:ssa-inference}

In some IRs, types are inferred rather than annotated. The SSA form makes type inference easier because each variable has a single definition, so its type can be determined from the defining expression.

