\section{Type Information for Optimization}\label{sec:type-optimization}

Type information enables a wide range of optimizations.

\subsection{Type-Specialized Operations}\label{sec:type-specialized-ops}

When the type of an operand is known, the compiler can select a specialized operation:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Generic:
  %result = call @add(%a, %b)     // dynamic dispatch

  // Specialized (type known):
  %result = add i32 %a, %b        // direct i32 add instruction
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Range Analysis}\label{sec:range-analysis}

Type information can be refined with range information:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Type: i32 (range: -2^31 to 2^31-1)
  // Refined type: i32 [0, 100] (range: 0 to 100)
  // Optimization: use unsigned comparison, or smaller integer type
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Nullability Analysis}\label{sec:nullability}

Type information about nullability enables null-check elimination:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  x : NonNull Ptr = get_ptr()
  *x = 42  // null check can be eliminated (x is known non-null)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Algebraic Types}\label{sec:algebraic-types}

For algebraic data types, the IR can represent the tag and payload explicitly:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  data Maybe a = Nothing | Just a

  // IR:
  // Maybe i32 is represented as:
  // tag: i1 (0 = Nothing, 1 = Just)
  // payload: i32 (valid only if tag = 1)
\end{verbatim}

