\section{Type-Safe IR Transformations}\label{sec:type-safe-transforms}

Typed IRs enable type-safe transformations: transformations that are guaranteed to produce well-typed output.

\subsection{Typed Rewriting}\label{sec:typed-rewriting}

Rewrite rules in a typed IR must preserve types:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Type-preserving rewrite:
  add i32 %x, 0 => %x           // identity: result type is i32, same as %x
  add i32 %x, %y => add i32 %y, %x  // commutativity: same type

  // Type-violating rewrite (REJECTED):
  add i32 %x, %y => %x          // WRONG: drops %y, but result type is still i32
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{E-graphs and Equality Saturation}\label{sec:egraphs}

Equality saturation with e-graphs~\cite{willsey2021} maintains a set of equivalent expressions, all of the same type. The e-graph ensures that rewrites preserve types.

\subsection{Certified Transformations}\label{sec:certified}

In a certified compiler, each transformation is accompanied by a proof that it preserves semantics. Type information is essential for these proofs: the type of the output must match the type of the input.

