\section{Examples: Typed LLVM, Cranelift Types}\label{sec:typed-examples}

\subsection{LLVM IR Types}\label{sec:llvm-types}

LLVM IR is a typed SSA IR with a rich type system:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Integer types:
  %x = add i32 %a, %b       ; 32-bit integer addition
  %y = add i64 %c, %d       ; 64-bit integer addition

  ; Floating-point types:
  %f = fadd float %a, %b    ; 32-bit float addition
  %g = fadd double %c, %d   ; 64-bit double addition

  ; Pointer types:
  %p = load i32*, i32** %pp  ; load a pointer to i32

  ; Vector types:
  %v = add <4 x i32> %a, %b  ; SIMD: 4 x 32-bit integer addition

  ; Struct types:
  %s = insertvalue {i32, float} %struct, i32 %val, 0
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Cranelift Types}\label{sec:cranelift-types}

Cranelift (the code generator for Wasmtime and other projects) uses a simpler type system:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Cranelift types:
  ; Integer: i8, i16, i32, i64
  ; Float: f32, f64
  ; Vector: i8x16, i16x8, i32x4, i64x2, f32x4, f64x2
  ; Reference: r32, r64 (opaque reference types)

  v0 = iadd v1, v2          ; integer add (type inferred from operands)
  v3 = fadd v4, v5          ; float add
\end{verbatim}

Cranelift's type system is designed for simplicity and speed: type checking is fast, and the type system is expressive enough for WebAssembly and similar languages.

\subsection{Comparison}\label{sec:typed-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Typed IR comparison}\label{tab:typed-ir-comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{LLVM} & \textbf{Cranelift} & \textbf{TAL} \\
\hline
SSA form & Yes & Yes & Yes \\
Type system & Rich & Simple & Rich (dependent) \\
Type checking & Full & Full & Full (certified) \\
Vector types & Yes & Yes & No \\
Pointer types & Yes & No (explicit) & Yes \\
Struct types & Yes & No & Yes \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{historical}
Typed IRs have their roots in the typed assembly language (TAL) work of Morrisett et al.~\cite{morrisett1999}, which demonstrated that type-preserving compilation is feasible and beneficial. LLVM~\cite{lattner2004} popularized typed SSA IRs in production compilers, with its rich type system enabling aggressive optimization. Cranelift~\cite{cranelift2024} demonstrated that a simpler type system can be sufficient for specific use cases (WebAssembly compilation). The ongoing trend is toward IRs that balance type expressiveness (for optimization) with simplicity (for fast compilation).
\end{historical}

