\section{Type Checking the IR}\label{sec:ir-type-checking}

A typed IR must be type-checked to ensure that all transformations preserve well-typedness. This is typically done as a verification pass after each transformation.

\subsection{Verification Pass}\label{sec:verification-pass}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{IR Type Verification}\label{alg:ir-verify}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Verify}{$\mathrm{IR}$}
  \For{each function $f$ in $\mathrm{IR}$}
    \State $\Gamma \gets \mathrm{parameter\_types}(f)$
    \For{each block $B$ in $f$}
      \For{each instruction $I$ in $B$}
        \If{$I$ is an assignment $x = e$}
          \State $\tau \gets \mathrm{type\_of}(e, \Gamma)$
          \State \textbf{assert} $\Gamma(x) = \tau$
          \State $\Gamma \gets \Gamma[x \mapsto \tau]$
        \ElsIf{$I$ is a phi $x = \phi(x_1, \ldots, x_n)$}
          \State $\tau \gets \Gamma(x_1)$
          \For{$i = 2$ to $n$}
            \State \textbf{assert} $\Gamma(x_i) = \tau$
          \EndFor
          \State \textbf{assert} $\Gamma(x) = \tau$
        \ElsIf{$I$ is a return $e$}
          \State \textbf{assert} $\mathrm{type\_of}(e, \Gamma) = \mathrm{return\_type}(f)$
        \EndIf
      \EndFor
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Lightweight Verification}\label{sec:lightweight-verify}

Full type verification can be expensive for large programs. Lightweight verification checks only the properties that are most likely to be violated:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{SSA property.} Every variable is defined exactly once.
  \item \textbf{Type consistency.} Every use of a variable has a type compatible with its definition.
  \item \textbf{Phi consistency.} Every phi function's arguments have the same type.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Debugging with Types}\label{sec:debug-types}

Type information in the IR is invaluable for debugging compiler bugs. When a transformation produces ill-typed output, the type checker identifies the specific instruction that violates the type rules, narrowing down the source of the bug.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Error message from IR type checker:
  error: instruction %5 = add i64 %3, %4 has type mismatch
    %3 has type i32 (defined at block B2, instruction 3)
    %4 has type i64 (defined at block B1, instruction 7)
    expected: both operands of add must have the same type
\end{verbatim}

