\section{Advanced Type-Preserving Compilation and Examples}\label{sec:typed-ir-advanced}

\subsection{Type-Preserving Compilation Pipeline}\label{sec:tpc-pipeline}

A complete type-preserving compiler maintains type information through every phase of compilation. The TIL compiler~\cite{morrisett1999} demonstrated this approach from System~F to typed assembly language.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Type-preserving compilation pipeline:
  //
  // Phase 1: Source -> Typed CPS
  //   - Convert to continuation-passing style
  //   - Types are preserved: (A -> B) becomes (A -> (B -> void) -> void)
  //
  // Phase 2: Typed CPS -> Typed Closure Conversion
  //   - Convert free variables to explicit environment parameters
  //   - Types: {fn: A * Env -> B, env: Env}
  //
  // Phase 3: Typed Closure Conversion -> Typed Hoisting
  //   - Hoist closures to top-level functions
  //   - Types preserved by adding environment parameters
  //
  // Phase 4: Typed Hoisting -> Typed Assembly Language (TAL)
  //   - Map to machine instructions with type annotations
  //   - Each register has a type; each instruction is type-checked
\end{verbatim}

\begin{definition}[Typed Assembly Language]\label{def:tal}
Typed Assembly Language (TAL) extends assembly language with types:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each register is annotated with a type.
  \item Each instruction is type-checked before execution.
  \item Labels are annotated with the types of their expected register state.
  \item Well-typed TAL programs cannot perform type-unsafe operations.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Typed assembly example:
  add: { r1: int, r2: int }
    r3 = r1 + r2        // type: r3 : int
    return { r3: int }

  // The type checker verifies:
  // - r1 and r2 are both int (required by add instruction)
  // - r3 has type int (result of int addition)
  // - The return type matches the label annotation
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Type Information for Advanced Optimizations}\label{sec:type-advanced-opts}

Rich type information enables optimizations beyond simple type specialization:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Optimization 1: Algebraic data type optimization
  // Source: data Option<T> = None | Some(T)
  // IR: Option<i32> = { tag: i1, payload: i32 }
  // Optimization: if None is rare, use null pointer optimization
  // IR': Option<i32> = ptr (null = None, non-null = Some)

  // Optimization 2: Newtype elimination
  // Source: struct Meters(f64); struct Seconds(f64);
  // IR: Meters and Seconds are both f64
  // Optimization: eliminate the newtype wrappers entirely

  // Optimization 3: Enum layout optimization
  // Source: enum Result<T, E> = Ok(T) | Err(E)
  // IR: Result<i32, String> = { tag: i1, data: union { i32, String } }
  // Optimization: if one variant is much larger, use niche optimization
  // (e.g., Option<&T> uses null pointer niche for None)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Dependent Types in IR}\label{sec:dependent-types-ir}

Some IRs use dependent types to express invariants that enable optimization:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Dependent type in IR:
  %A : memref<N x f32>   // N is a runtime value but the type tracks it
  %B : memref<N x f32>   // Same N: compiler knows dimensions match

  // This enables:
  // - Eliminating bounds checks (dimensions are known to match)
  // - Fusing operations (shapes are known to be compatible)
  // - Parallelizing (dimensions enable decomposition)
\end{verbatim}

