\subsection{Type-Directed Register Allocation}\label{sec:type-regalloc}

Type information can guide register allocation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type-based register classes.} Integer values go in integer registers; float values go in floating-point registers; vector values go in SIMD registers.
  \item \textbf{Lifetime analysis.} Values with longer lifetimes are assigned to callee-saved registers; short-lived values use caller-saved registers.
  \item \textbf{Type-specialized spilling.} When spilling to the stack, the type determines the store/load instructions (e.g., \texttt{store i32} vs. \texttt{store double}).
  \item \textbf{Coalescing.} Values of the same type that are live at the same time can share registers through coalescing.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Type-directed register allocation:
  // %a : i32 -> allocated to eax (integer register)
  // %b : f64 -> allocated to xmm0 (SSE register)
  // %c : <4 x i32> -> allocated to ymm0 (AVX register)
  //
  // The type determines the register class, which determines
  // which physical registers are available for allocation.
\end{verbatim}

