\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-61}

Typed IRs carry type information throughout the compilation pipeline, enabling type-preserving compilation, type-specialized optimization, and type-safe transformations. The choice of type system (rich vs. simple) depends on the compiler's goals: a rich type system enables more optimizations but slows compilation; a simple type system is fast but limits optimization opportunities.

Type verification after each transformation ensures that the compiler maintains well-typedness throughout the pipeline. This is essential for compiler correctness and provides valuable debugging information when transformations go wrong.

For further reading, we recommend Morrisett et al.'s TAL paper~\cite{morrisett1999}, the LLVM language reference~\cite{llvm2024}, and the Cranelift documentation~\cite{cranelift2024}. The work by Benton et al.~\cite{benton2015} on a fully verified type-preserving compiler demonstrates the state of the art in certified compilation.
